elgrep (eg) !!VERSION!!
!!AUTHORS!!

elgrep (eg) recursively searches the current directory for lines matching a
regex pattern. It carries the search path of ripgrep, by Andrew Gallant, and
puts a sparse n-gram index in front of it. The index picks candidate files
and the regex engine verifies them, so results match a plain scan exactly.

Searches use the index by default. The first search under a directory builds
the index and waits for it; eg-indexd then watches the tree and refreshes the
index in the background, so later searches start warm. Pass --no-index to
skip all of that and scan every file.

A query the index cannot serve, such as an inverted match or a read from
stdin, fails with an explanation instead of quietly changing what you get.
Every such message names the flag that runs the query anyway.

Use -h for short descriptions and --help for more details.

Project home page: https://github.com/eliosai/sngram

USAGE:
    eg [OPTIONS] PATTERN [PATH ...]
    eg [OPTIONS] -e PATTERN ... [PATH ...]
    eg [OPTIONS] -f PATTERNFILE ... [PATH ...]
    eg [OPTIONS] --files [PATH ...]
    eg [OPTIONS] --type-list
    command | eg [OPTIONS] --no-index PATTERN
    eg [OPTIONS] --help
    eg [OPTIONS] --version

POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS:
    <PATTERN>
        A regular expression used for searching. To match a pattern beginning
        with a dash, use the -e/--regexp flag.

        For example, to search for the literal '-foo', you can use this flag:

            eg -e -foo

        You can also use the special '--' delimiter to indicate that no more
        flags will be provided. Namely, the following is equivalent to the
        above:

            eg -- -foo

    <PATH>...
        A file or directory to search. Directories are searched recursively.
        File paths specified on the command line override glob and ignore
        rules.

INPUT OPTIONS:
